WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:02.475
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:05.450 --> 00:00:07.340
NARRATOR: After
water, tea is the most

00:00:07.340 --> 00:00:09.350
consumed beverage in the world.

00:00:09.350 --> 00:00:11.450
The different types are
determined by the degree

00:00:11.450 --> 00:00:13.820
to which the producer
lets the harvested leaves

00:00:13.820 --> 00:00:16.490
oxidize before drying them.

00:00:16.490 --> 00:00:19.910
Green tea, for example, is
barely fermented, whereas

00:00:19.910 --> 00:00:22.100
black tea is greatly fermented.

00:00:22.100 --> 00:00:23.765
Oolong falls
somewhere in between.

00:00:25.002 --> 00:00:27.950
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:27.950 --> 00:00:31.340
In Chinese, oolong
means black dragon.

00:00:31.340 --> 00:00:35.450
It's a semi-oxidized tea
fermented more than green tea,

00:00:35.450 --> 00:00:37.880
yet less than black tea.

00:00:37.880 --> 00:00:41.960
It's taste, aroma, and color,
from light yellow to dark red,

00:00:41.960 --> 00:00:45.500
vary according to how the
tea leaves are processed.

00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:47.990
This amber oolong
produced in Thailand

00:00:47.990 --> 00:00:49.985
has a rich, smooth
roasted taste.

00:00:53.290 --> 00:00:56.230
Like most teas, oolong
is made from the leaves

00:00:56.230 --> 00:01:00.010
of a flowering plants species
called Camellia sinensis.

00:01:00.010 --> 00:01:01.750
Harvest time is
during the plant's

00:01:01.750 --> 00:01:03.970
peak growing season,
which in Thailand

00:01:03.970 --> 00:01:05.170
is from May to November.

00:01:08.970 --> 00:01:11.730
Workers handpicked what's
known as the flush.

00:01:11.730 --> 00:01:14.370
A grouping of two young
lives and a bud, which

00:01:14.370 --> 00:01:16.020
grows out the top of the plant.

00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:20.790
At this time of year, the
plant produces a new flush

00:01:20.790 --> 00:01:22.230
every seven to 15 days.

00:01:27.030 --> 00:01:29.820
An experienced tea master
directs every phase

00:01:29.820 --> 00:01:32.430
of the process, the
first step of which

00:01:32.430 --> 00:01:35.340
is called solar withering.

00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:37.320
Workers bring the
leaves into a glass

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:39.990
roofed building then
spread them out in the sun

00:01:39.990 --> 00:01:42.120
for 15 to 20 minutes.

00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:45.660
This kick starts the oxidation
fermentation process,

00:01:45.660 --> 00:01:48.840
as the chlorophyll enzymes
inside the wilting leaves

00:01:48.840 --> 00:01:51.030
start breaking down.

00:01:51.030 --> 00:01:55.055
At the same time, the moisture
inside begins evaporating.

00:01:56.420 --> 00:01:59.060
Workers keep moving the
leaves around to ensure

00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:00.830
a thorough exposure to the sun.

00:02:05.970 --> 00:02:10.470
Then they gather up the leaves
for step 2, indoor withering.

00:02:10.470 --> 00:02:14.040
The leaves lie on bamboo
trays for six to eight hours,

00:02:14.040 --> 00:02:17.775
where gently stirred every two
hours, they oxidize further.

00:02:19.810 --> 00:02:22.090
Step 3, disruption.

00:02:22.090 --> 00:02:25.210
The leaves go into
a rotating drum.

00:02:25.210 --> 00:02:27.910
As they tumble, they
bruise and tear.

00:02:27.910 --> 00:02:30.460
This breaks down the
cell structures enabling

00:02:30.460 --> 00:02:33.340
oxygen to penetrate
deep inside, greatly

00:02:33.340 --> 00:02:35.750
accelerating fermentation.

00:02:35.750 --> 00:02:38.620
This also releases the
leaf juices which helps

00:02:38.620 --> 00:02:40.090
throw out the taste of the tea.

00:02:44.350 --> 00:02:47.020
When the tea master determines
the leaves have sufficiently

00:02:47.020 --> 00:02:50.410
oxidized, they stop
the oxidation process

00:02:50.410 --> 00:02:52.390
by tumbling the leaves
and a gas heated

00:02:52.390 --> 00:02:55.420
dryer for 10 to 15 minutes.

00:02:55.420 --> 00:02:58.870
This fourth step of the
process is called fixation,

00:02:58.870 --> 00:03:02.620
because it fixes the oxidation
at the desired level, which

00:03:02.620 --> 00:03:06.820
can be anywhere from 8% to
85%, depending on the variety

00:03:06.820 --> 00:03:09.610
of oolong in production.

00:03:09.610 --> 00:03:12.340
This is the most critical
part of the process,

00:03:12.340 --> 00:03:15.880
because it determines the
team's taste, aroma, and color.

00:03:21.010 --> 00:03:24.550
The next step forms the tea
leaves into tiny pellets.

00:03:24.550 --> 00:03:27.760
First, workers shake the
leaves on a sieve to filter

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:29.200
out the dust-like particles.

00:03:33.070 --> 00:03:35.620
Then they bag the
leaves in a cotton cloth

00:03:35.620 --> 00:03:38.530
and place them first in
a kneading machine then

00:03:38.530 --> 00:03:39.985
afterward in a rolling press.

00:03:42.990 --> 00:03:45.510
Kneading and rolling the
back twists the leaves

00:03:45.510 --> 00:03:46.875
inside into tiny pellets.

00:03:48.660 --> 00:03:51.450
Forming these pellet shapes
intensifies the flavor

00:03:51.450 --> 00:03:54.510
of the tea, and when the
tea is steeped in hot water,

00:03:54.510 --> 00:03:55.980
releases that flavor slowly.

00:03:55.980 --> 00:03:58.710
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:04:02.080 --> 00:04:04.720
They repeat the sieve,
kneading, and rolling cycle up

00:04:04.720 --> 00:04:15.050
to 35 times until the tea master
is satisfied with the result.

00:04:15.050 --> 00:04:17.895
Then and only then does
the final step begin.

00:04:17.895 --> 00:04:18.695
Firing.

00:04:20.480 --> 00:04:23.780
They transfer the tea to an
oven in which it undergoes three

00:04:23.780 --> 00:04:27.110
drying cycles of 20 minutes
each at a temperature

00:04:27.110 --> 00:04:30.188
of approximately 210
degrees Fahrenheit.

00:04:32.820 --> 00:04:35.270
This dries the
damp tea, reducing

00:04:35.270 --> 00:04:39.410
the moisture content to the
target level of less than 5%.

00:04:39.410 --> 00:04:41.750
The firing also brings
out the fragrance.

00:04:44.020 --> 00:04:47.500
The traditional way to brew
oolong is in a clay teapot,

00:04:47.500 --> 00:04:50.260
using about 2 teaspoons
of tea per cup.

00:04:50.260 --> 00:04:55.330
Ideally, the water should be
190 to 210 degrees Fahrenheit.

00:04:55.330 --> 00:04:57.760
Steeping time is
from 3 to 10 minutes,

00:04:57.760 --> 00:05:00.190
and you can brew the same
leaves up to five times.

00:05:00.190 --> 00:05:03.240
[MUSIC PLAYING]

